<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "ldm-unraid">
<!ENTITY author    "emaspa">
<!ENTITY version   "0.1.2">
<!ENTITY launch    "Main">
<!ENTITY repo      "emaspa/ldm-unraid">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/&repo;/main/ldm-unraid.plg">
<!ENTITY support   "https://forums.unraid.net/topic/201053-plugin-support-windows-dynamic-disks-ldm-unraid/">
<!ENTITY pkgURL    "https://github.com/&repo;/releases/download/v&version;/&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz">
<!ENTITY md5       "5877e3f69ce7d58565809db7725f25af">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" min="7.0.0" support="&support;" description="Read Windows dynamic disks (LDM): simple, spanned, striped and mirrored volumes, mounted under /mnt/disks and optionally shared over SMB.">

<CHANGES>
### 0.1.2
- SMB share comment: control characters and backslashes in a disk's group or volume name are replaced with _, so a crafted disk can't add lines to the share's Samba config

### 0.1.1
- Plugin icon on the Plugins page, package description
- Install keeps host directory symlinks and permissions (TAR_OPTIONS, as in the other plugins)

### 0.1.0
- First release: find Windows dynamic disks, assemble simple, spanned, striped and mirrored volumes with device-mapper, and mount them read-only (or read-write) under /mnt/disks
- Windows Dynamic Disks section on the Main page and an ldmctl command-line tool
- Share a mounted volume over SMB, public or per user, from the Main page or with ldmctl share
</CHANGES>

<README>
**Windows Dynamic Disks** reads volumes from disks that Windows converted to dynamic (LDM): simple, spanned, striped and mirrored volumes on MBR or GPT disks.
Volumes are assembled with device-mapper and mounted under /mnt/disks, read-only by default. Nothing is written to the disks unless you choose Mount R/W.
</README>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz">
<URL>&pkgURL;</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# Keep host directory symlinks and permissions (the package has no directory
# headers, and these options keep it that way if one ever slips in).
TAR_OPTIONS="--keep-directory-symlink --no-overwrite-dir" \
  upgradepkg --install-new /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# Drop packages left over from older versions on the flash drive.
find /boot/config/plugins/&name;/ -maxdepth 1 -name '&name;-*.txz' ! -name '&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz' -delete
echo "&name; &version; installed. Volumes appear under Main, Windows Dynamic Disks, and in 'ldmctl status'."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/scripts/ldmctl unmount-all
removepkg &name;-&version;-x86_64-1
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name; /tmp/&name;
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/&name;
echo "&name; removed."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
